00:01:59.416 --> 00:02:00.446
Hopefully you're aware of, you know,

00:02:00.446 --> 00:02:02.456
we have pretty much every subject in grade

00:02:02.456 --> 00:02:05.176
starting from pre-K through
the core of college,

00:02:05.176 --> 00:02:08.416
we have Khan Academy Kids
that goes from a third grade

00:02:08.416 --> 00:02:11.646
or a three year old to
six or seven year olds.

00:02:11.646 --> 00:02:13.676
And that's reading, writing, math

00:02:13.676 --> 00:02:15.006
and social emotional learning.

00:02:15.006 --> 00:02:16.556
Everything I'm talking about is free,

00:02:16.556 --> 00:02:19.156
not for profit, paid
for with those donations

00:02:19.156 --> 00:02:21.666
which hopefully some of y'all are making.

00:02:21.666 --> 00:02:23.976
We have math that obviously
goes well into elementary,

00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:25.386
Middle High School and then college,

00:02:25.386 --> 00:02:27.566
statistics, calculus
and even multi-variable.

00:02:27.566 --> 00:02:30.106
We're about to launch a
multi-variable calculus

00:02:30.106 --> 00:02:32.946
that has exercises in
the next couple of weeks.

00:02:32.946 --> 00:02:34.136
We've also launched something called

00:02:34.136 --> 00:02:35.906
Get Ready for Grade Level courses.

00:02:35.906 --> 00:02:37.546
This is an issue that's always existed,

00:02:37.546 --> 00:02:40.736
but it's frankly a bigger
issue now because of COVID.

00:02:40.736 --> 00:02:42.736
That as kids come into
the new school year,

00:02:42.736 --> 00:02:44.326
as you come into the new school year,

00:02:44.326 --> 00:02:45.296
how do you know that you're ready

00:02:45.296 --> 00:02:46.916
for whatever course you're getting into,

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:48.876
you can go to the Get
Ready for, let's say,

00:02:48.876 --> 00:02:51.736
sixth grade course so they
Get Ready for algebra course,

00:02:51.736 --> 00:02:52.746
take the course challenge.

00:02:52.746 --> 00:02:55.046
If you do well on it, that
means you're pretty much ready.

00:02:55.046 --> 00:02:57.666
If you don't do well on
it, then you can finish

00:02:57.666 --> 00:03:00.056
and get mastery on that Get
Ready for Grade Level course,

00:03:00.056 --> 00:03:01.676
it'll accelerate you
through the stuff you know,

00:03:01.676 --> 00:03:03.606
by taking course
challenges and unit tests.

00:03:03.606 --> 00:03:04.439
But at the other stuff,

00:03:04.439 --> 00:03:06.216
you can make sure you
have a strong foundation,

00:03:06.216 --> 00:03:08.746
which you can do in parallel
to the grade level course.

00:03:08.746 --> 00:03:10.036
Or, you can do before

00:03:10.036 --> 00:03:12.176
so that you have a
really strong foundation.

00:03:12.176 --> 00:03:14.706
Another project I wanna make
sure that everyone knows about

00:03:14.706 --> 00:03:16.396
before I jump into the questions.

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:18.746
There's a side project that I hope one day

00:03:18.746 --> 00:03:20.176
might become part of Khan Academy

00:03:20.176 --> 00:03:22.286
but today it is a side project.

00:03:22.286 --> 00:03:24.816
It's called Schoolhouse.world.

00:03:24.816 --> 00:03:28.086
And this is a place where you
as a student or your children,

00:03:28.086 --> 00:03:32.806
or anyone you know, can get
free live group tutoring

00:03:32.806 --> 00:03:37.526
from really well, from
accomplished and really good tutors

00:03:37.526 --> 00:03:39.656
of frankly all ages and
if you wanna be a tutor,

00:03:39.656 --> 00:03:42.016
you can also apply to be a tutor.

00:03:42.016 --> 00:03:45.866
And right now, we have more
tutors and less students.

00:03:45.866 --> 00:03:48.426
So, there's actually
several thousand slots

00:03:48.426 --> 00:03:50.946
of where you can get free tutoring

00:03:50.946 --> 00:03:52.666
especially we're starting
in high school math.

00:03:52.666 --> 00:03:53.936
And then in the next few weeks,

00:03:53.936 --> 00:03:56.186
we're probably going to
expand to K through 12 math,

00:03:56.186 --> 00:03:58.386
the sciences, we're also
doing SAT in math now,

00:03:58.386 --> 00:04:01.396
we'll probably do SAT tutoring in English

00:04:01.396 --> 00:04:02.406
in the next few weeks as well.

00:04:02.406 --> 00:04:06.636
So, of any age, if you're a
parent of a younger child,

00:04:06.636 --> 00:04:08.546
say 13 or under, you'd be the person

00:04:08.546 --> 00:04:10.526
who would have to go register
on Schoolhouse.world,

00:04:10.526 --> 00:04:12.826
but encourage as many folks
as possible to go there,

00:04:12.826 --> 00:04:14.006
you can kinda get on the waiting list,

00:04:14.006 --> 00:04:15.126
but there's a lot of openings

00:04:15.126 --> 00:04:17.266
for several thousands of folks right now

00:04:17.266 --> 00:04:22.016
to start getting some free
group tutoring sessions live.

00:04:22.016 --> 00:04:25.586
So with that, happy to
start taking some questions.

00:04:25.586 --> 00:04:27.799
So, let's see from YouTube.

00:04:28.886 --> 00:04:30.453
80 videos, there are
actually two questions.

00:04:30.453 --> 00:04:32.976
"How can we get interested
in studying on our own?"

00:04:32.976 --> 00:04:34.916
And 80 videos also says,
"what is the easiest way

00:04:34.916 --> 00:04:37.116
to get interested in math?"

00:04:37.116 --> 00:04:40.996
Well, I'll try to get
you interested right now.

00:04:40.996 --> 00:04:44.166
You know, every subject
that you look at in school,

00:04:44.166 --> 00:04:45.323
sometimes when you're
in school, you're like,

00:04:45.323 --> 00:04:46.656
"Oh, this is being forced on me,

00:04:46.656 --> 00:04:47.806
I have to do this for the test."

00:04:47.806 --> 00:04:51.206
And when you think of it
that way, it loses sometimes

00:04:51.206 --> 00:04:52.926
sometimes it might lose its joy.

00:04:52.926 --> 00:04:56.166
But when you appreciate
that, as, you know,

00:04:56.166 --> 00:04:58.386
as sentient beings in this universe,

00:04:58.386 --> 00:05:00.826
one of the things that
makes humans humans is that

00:05:00.826 --> 00:05:03.006
we're trying to understand
the universe we're living in,

00:05:03.006 --> 00:05:03.986
and we're trying to understand

00:05:03.986 --> 00:05:07.676
how do we interact with
society and the universe,

00:05:07.676 --> 00:05:10.066
and have happy, productive lives.

00:05:10.066 --> 00:05:11.926
And so, at the end of the day, knowledge

00:05:11.926 --> 00:05:14.996
is really the underlying
thing that gives us clues

00:05:14.996 --> 00:05:17.656
about the mystery we're in and
how we can interact with it.

00:05:17.656 --> 00:05:19.676
And, the things that
you see in your textbook

00:05:19.676 --> 00:05:21.526
or on Khan Academy or wherever else.

00:05:21.526 --> 00:05:24.496
This is human knowledge
that has been accumulated

00:05:24.496 --> 00:05:26.626
over thousands of years.

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:28.906
People have oftentimes
dedicated their entire lives

00:05:28.906 --> 00:05:30.656
to one paragraph of your textbook

00:05:30.656 --> 00:05:33.416
and you get to just see
the answer or the solution,

00:05:33.416 --> 00:05:35.566
or the understanding right there.

00:05:35.566 --> 00:05:38.506
And so, as you read your textbook
or as you learn in class,

00:05:38.506 --> 00:05:41.296
or as you're on Khan
Academy, remind yourself

00:05:41.296 --> 00:05:44.046
how lucky we are to have this knowledge.

00:05:44.046 --> 00:05:47.376
And then also, never
be satisfied with just,

00:05:47.376 --> 00:05:50.476
you know memorizing the
formula or figuring out

00:05:50.476 --> 00:05:52.596
how to pattern match for the test.

00:05:52.596 --> 00:05:54.266
Always think about how does this connect

00:05:54.266 --> 00:05:55.766
to other things that I know?

00:05:55.766 --> 00:05:59.326
How does this connect to my
understanding of the universe?

00:05:59.326 --> 00:06:00.956
You know, when you, if
you're in chemistry class,

00:06:00.956 --> 00:06:04.226
and you're learning
about atoms and electrons

00:06:04.226 --> 00:06:05.846
and protons and neutrons you realize,

00:06:05.846 --> 00:06:09.196
wow, these are mental constructs
that we as human beings

00:06:09.196 --> 00:06:13.596
have formed to predict what
happens when these substances

00:06:13.596 --> 00:06:15.866
interact with each other
in different conditions.

00:06:15.866 --> 00:06:16.956
But what really is an atom?

00:06:16.956 --> 00:06:19.286
Like, you know, if we can't
see it, if we can't touch it,

00:06:19.286 --> 00:06:21.716
we can measure it in indirect ways.

00:06:21.716 --> 00:06:22.886
You know, what does that really mean?

00:06:22.886 --> 00:06:24.146
And, you know, what is real

00:06:24.146 --> 00:06:27.136
if an atom is mostly open, free space.

00:06:27.136 --> 00:06:29.746
If most of what we see around
ourselves is actually vacuum

00:06:29.746 --> 00:06:32.996
and what I think is
solid is actually really

00:06:32.996 --> 00:06:36.236
coulomb repulsive forces
between atoms that

00:06:36.236 --> 00:06:38.606
don't want their electrons
to overlap with each other.

00:06:38.606 --> 00:06:40.006
When you start to realize
like, wow, this is starting

00:06:40.006 --> 00:06:42.564
to explain the philosophy of the universe

00:06:42.564 --> 00:06:44.756
and, you know, you can
do that in any science.

00:06:44.756 --> 00:06:46.616
You know, the humanities give us clues

00:06:46.616 --> 00:06:49.586
about the human condition
and who are we as as beings,

00:06:49.586 --> 00:06:52.516
and math, you know, there's
a special place for math

00:06:52.516 --> 00:06:55.846
where that's the purest way
of understanding the universe.

00:06:55.846 --> 00:06:58.136
If we ever come in contact with aliens,

00:06:58.136 --> 00:06:59.686
that try to communicate with us,

00:06:59.686 --> 00:07:02.956
math is going to be
our initial touch point

00:07:02.956 --> 00:07:05.946
where we are able to
establish that the other party

00:07:05.946 --> 00:07:08.466
is actually a sentient intelligent being,

00:07:08.466 --> 00:07:09.299
is probably with math.

00:07:09.299 --> 00:07:11.006
We'll probably try to, you
know, give them prime numbers

00:07:11.006 --> 00:07:13.236
or something like that, or they
would give us prime numbers

00:07:13.236 --> 00:07:16.896
to see whether we're actually
you know, intelligent

00:07:16.896 --> 00:07:19.876
or some other type of
universal mathematical problem.

00:07:19.876 --> 00:07:23.116
So, you know, I find math to
be the purest, most abstract...

00:07:23.116 --> 00:07:26.726
everything else is kind
of clouded by our senses.

00:07:26.726 --> 00:07:29.216
You know, even things like
physics and other sciences.

00:07:29.216 --> 00:07:31.396
It's really all that we
can perceive and measure,

00:07:31.396 --> 00:07:33.696
but math has a purity
to it that gets kind of,

00:07:36.276 --> 00:07:37.626
at a much deeper state.

00:07:37.626 --> 00:07:38.946
And, you know, there's definitely

00:07:38.946 --> 00:07:40.276
some concepts out there in math

00:07:40.276 --> 00:07:41.443
that I genuinely think
will blow your mind.

00:07:41.443 --> 00:07:42.906
Wonder if y'all have all seen that video

00:07:42.906 --> 00:07:44.836
where I talk about Euler's identity

00:07:44.836 --> 00:07:49.036
where e to the i pi is
equal to negative one.

00:07:49.036 --> 00:07:49.869
And that's one of those things,

00:07:49.869 --> 00:07:50.966
that it just gives you chills

00:07:50.966 --> 00:07:54.086
when you realize that e comes
from notions of continuously

00:07:54.086 --> 00:07:56.436
compounding interest, the
derivative of a function

00:07:56.436 --> 00:08:01.156
that's itself comes out of
some natural phenomenon,

00:08:01.156 --> 00:08:02.319
exponential growth.

00:08:03.196 --> 00:08:05.406
Pi, obviously, the ratio
of the circumference

00:08:05.406 --> 00:08:08.336
is diameter of a circle,
i is a number that,

00:08:08.336 --> 00:08:10.996
in some ways constructed by mathematicians

00:08:10.996 --> 00:08:13.566
to have a, what could be the net squared,

00:08:13.566 --> 00:08:15.286
defined as the square
root of negative one,

00:08:15.286 --> 00:08:17.496
and the fact that there's
a very strong argument

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:19.666
that e to the i pi, these
things that come out

00:08:19.666 --> 00:08:23.867
of completely different
domains equals negative one,

00:08:23.867 --> 00:08:27.146
it's chilling, it's like
there's some hidden pattern

00:08:27.146 --> 00:08:31.203
to the universe that we're
just getting glimpses of,

00:08:31.203 --> 00:08:33.136
you know, some people have
called Euler's identity

00:08:33.136 --> 00:08:36.386
proof of the existence of God, you know,

00:08:36.386 --> 00:08:39.326
I'll leave it up to you to think about

00:08:39.326 --> 00:08:41.486
whether you feel that or not.

00:08:41.486 --> 00:08:44.556
But, I'm just trying to
make you appreciate that,

00:08:44.556 --> 00:08:46.966
you know, the tools that
you're building in math class

00:08:46.966 --> 00:08:50.396
are going to open up a
universe that will give you,

00:08:50.396 --> 00:08:51.946
that will be awe inspiring.

00:08:51.946 --> 00:08:54.496
And, they will give you
skills that will help you

00:08:54.496 --> 00:08:57.746
be really productive
and useful and in life

00:08:57.746 --> 00:08:59.116
and hopefully be able to support yourself

00:08:59.116 --> 00:09:00.826
and support those of you who care about

00:09:00.826 --> 00:09:02.066
and make a dent in the universe.

00:09:02.066 --> 00:09:05.239
So anyway, long answer, you've
triggered me in a good way.

00:09:06.096 --> 00:09:07.519
See other question.

00:09:08.646 --> 00:09:10.013
From YouTube, Vincer Gaming,

00:09:10.013 --> 00:09:12.986
"When did you really start love learning?"

00:09:12.986 --> 00:09:17.326
You know, you can tell from
my answer, I just gave.

00:09:17.326 --> 00:09:19.076
I love learning.

00:09:19.076 --> 00:09:21.216
Like one of the reasons why I love my job

00:09:21.216 --> 00:09:23.826
is a big part of my job is
I continue to make content

00:09:23.826 --> 00:09:26.056
and I continue to learn new things,

00:09:26.056 --> 00:09:28.336
so that I can really
intuitively understand them,

00:09:28.336 --> 00:09:29.596
connect it to other things that I know

00:09:29.596 --> 00:09:32.156
and hopefully communicate
it to y'all in ways

00:09:32.156 --> 00:09:33.876
that are useful and other parts of my job

00:09:33.876 --> 00:09:35.706
where we're growing an organization,

00:09:35.706 --> 00:09:38.506
we're building software,
we're creating new content.

00:09:38.506 --> 00:09:40.016
We're figuring out how
do we build awareness

00:09:40.016 --> 00:09:42.936
for what we're doing, there's
also learning that I get to do

00:09:42.936 --> 00:09:44.796
with really great people who teach me

00:09:44.796 --> 00:09:47.096
and I get to learn from
experience as well.

00:09:47.096 --> 00:09:51.236
I would say, you know,
early on, in my schooling,

00:09:51.236 --> 00:09:52.196
it was kind of hit and miss.

00:09:52.196 --> 00:09:54.796
Certain classes, I was
naturally excited about.

00:09:54.796 --> 00:09:57.066
And I think I was lucky to
have some really great teachers

00:09:57.066 --> 00:09:58.326
and then in other classes,

00:09:58.326 --> 00:10:01.006
they didn't resonate as strongly
with me from an early age.

00:10:01.006 --> 00:10:02.786
But then, by the time
I got to high school,

00:10:02.786 --> 00:10:05.026
especially college, I
started to appreciate

00:10:05.026 --> 00:10:07.546
how everything connected and
how empowering all of it was.

00:10:07.546 --> 00:10:10.356
And then, I realized that, you
know, some of that learning

00:10:10.356 --> 00:10:12.156
that I was doing for fun,
even outside of school

00:10:12.156 --> 00:10:13.386
was really connected to the learning

00:10:13.386 --> 00:10:14.426
that I was doing in school.

00:10:14.426 --> 00:10:16.406
So, I think it's been a journey for me.

00:10:16.406 --> 00:10:19.676
I think maybe when I was in my late teens

00:10:19.676 --> 00:10:23.489
that I really unabashedly
become someone who,

00:10:25.056 --> 00:10:28.006
you know, would very openly
say that I love learning,

00:10:28.006 --> 00:10:29.367
'cause I really do think

00:10:29.367 --> 00:10:32.796
it is the most human action we can do.

00:10:32.796 --> 00:10:33.629
It's you know, we've been given

00:10:33.629 --> 00:10:37.119
these 100 billion or so
neurons for a reason.

00:10:39.186 --> 00:10:43.618
So, from YouTube, another
question from Vincer.

00:10:43.618 --> 00:10:45.183
Actually, there's two
questions here from Vincer.

00:10:45.183 --> 00:10:46.996
"How should you prepare for a test?"

00:10:46.996 --> 00:10:48.496
And then from Nakheel Govenda,

00:10:49.398 --> 00:10:50.231
"I hope you're well."

00:10:50.231 --> 00:10:51.873
I'm doing great, thanks Nakheel.

00:10:51.873 --> 00:10:53.956
"What are the best
studying methods techniques

00:10:53.956 --> 00:10:57.006
to get a 90 and above
percentage in high school?"

00:10:57.006 --> 00:11:00.046
So, for the best way to prepare for a test

00:11:00.046 --> 00:11:03.546
is to be thinking about the
concepts well before the test.

00:11:03.546 --> 00:11:05.975
And look, I'll say this as someone,

00:11:05.975 --> 00:11:07.376
I still am a procrastinator sometimes,

00:11:07.376 --> 00:11:09.886
but when I was young,
a teenager, in college,

00:11:09.886 --> 00:11:11.736
I was a procrastinator.

00:11:11.736 --> 00:11:13.546
So, I'm not gonna judge any of you

00:11:13.546 --> 00:11:16.086
who are cramming for late night exams

00:11:16.086 --> 00:11:18.116
or, you know, trying to
learn as much as you can

00:11:18.116 --> 00:11:19.696
staying up all night, but I will tell you

00:11:19.696 --> 00:11:22.806
as kind of Uncle Sal, that is not healthy.

00:11:22.806 --> 00:11:26.116
And I've learned and if I
were to, if I had do overs,

00:11:26.116 --> 00:11:30.406
if you just kind of, you
know, do a reasonable amount

00:11:30.406 --> 00:11:31.816
every day and while you're doing it,

00:11:31.816 --> 00:11:34.176
just don't pattern match
and do it robotically.

00:11:34.176 --> 00:11:36.966
Really ponder it, think
about what you're working on.

00:11:36.966 --> 00:11:38.996
By the time the test comes around,

00:11:38.996 --> 00:11:41.426
you're gonna say like,
"Oh, this is intuitive."

00:11:41.426 --> 00:11:43.366
Because you're ti... your
brain will have had a chance

00:11:43.366 --> 00:11:46.436
to really marinate in the knowledge.

00:11:46.436 --> 00:11:47.573
And, you know, the one
thing I will tell you

00:11:47.573 --> 00:11:50.886
and this is a skill that I
learned actually in college.

00:11:50.886 --> 00:11:52.606
You know, what, I had
a degree in, I was...

00:11:52.606 --> 00:11:54.366
I majored in electro-engineering,
computer science

00:11:54.366 --> 00:11:55.806
and another in math.

00:11:55.806 --> 00:11:58.303
And, in some of those
theoretical math classes,

00:11:58.303 --> 00:12:00.986
you know, you look at the
problems that you're given,

00:12:00.986 --> 00:12:03.913
let's say a topology
class, and you're like,

00:12:03.913 --> 00:12:05.526
"Okay, I understand the problem,

00:12:05.526 --> 00:12:08.086
but I have no idea how to
approach this problem."

00:12:08.086 --> 00:12:11.556
And what I found is, if you
give your brain the problem,

00:12:11.556 --> 00:12:13.476
you think about it for a few minutes

00:12:13.476 --> 00:12:16.226
and then you kind of delegate the problem

00:12:16.226 --> 00:12:19.526
to your subconscious and
maybe even sleep on it,

00:12:19.526 --> 00:12:22.086
literally sleep on it, that the next day,

00:12:22.086 --> 00:12:25.246
all of a sudden your subconscious,
like passes you a memo

00:12:25.246 --> 00:12:27.336
like, hey, this is the answer.

00:12:27.336 --> 00:12:29.196
And you're like, oh, okay, yes, I know,

00:12:29.196 --> 00:12:30.999
I can now do that topological proof.

00:12:32.121 --> 00:12:34.326
And, so I learned in undergraduate,

00:12:34.326 --> 00:12:36.376
you know, essentially
having challenges like this

00:12:36.376 --> 00:12:38.516
that like, we have an
incredible, you know,

00:12:38.516 --> 00:12:41.276
our consciousness, the part
that we strongly associate with

00:12:41.276 --> 00:12:44.906
is just a small part of the
horsepower that you have.

00:12:44.906 --> 00:12:46.586
And I truly believe everyone has.

00:12:46.586 --> 00:12:49.230
I mean, these hundreds
of billions of neurons

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:51.486
that we have working for us.

00:12:51.486 --> 00:12:55.096
And so, you know, learn
to trust and delegate.

00:12:55.096 --> 00:12:57.506
So, the way that you do that
is you prime your brain,

00:12:57.506 --> 00:12:59.846
you take the knowledge in, you read it,

00:12:59.846 --> 00:13:01.526
you try to get... maybe watch a video

00:13:01.526 --> 00:13:04.186
to try to get some practice,
maybe some of it sticks.

00:13:04.186 --> 00:13:06.146
Maybe some of it sticks
enough to do the problem,

00:13:06.146 --> 00:13:08.236
but you still don't have
a strong intuition yet.

00:13:08.236 --> 00:13:09.296
Let it marinate.

00:13:09.296 --> 00:13:11.616
If you don't know how to do
the problem, let it marinate.

00:13:11.616 --> 00:13:13.686
You'd be surprised 24 hours later,

00:13:13.686 --> 00:13:14.986
all of a sudden you know how to do it.

00:13:14.986 --> 00:13:15.966
Now, what I just described

00:13:15.966 --> 00:13:17.506
is not a great technique for cramming

00:13:17.506 --> 00:13:19.106
because it takes a little
bit of a leap of faith

00:13:19.106 --> 00:13:21.836
and sometimes it might not
hit for 24 hours or 48 hours.

00:13:21.836 --> 00:13:24.476
I'd be surprised if it actually
takes longer than that.

00:13:24.476 --> 00:13:27.816
But, it's all about
creating a habit of learning

00:13:27.816 --> 00:13:30.796
in whatever subject but
regardless of whatever timeframe

00:13:30.796 --> 00:13:33.316
you're doing it, whether
you're doing it in a healthy,

00:13:33.316 --> 00:13:35.356
you know, regular way or
whether you're cramming

00:13:35.356 --> 00:13:38.316
before a test, always
try to draw connections,

00:13:38.316 --> 00:13:41.636
always try to, just don't
assume that A leads to C,

00:13:41.636 --> 00:13:43.556
say why does A lead to C?

00:13:43.556 --> 00:13:45.126
Is it 'cause it leads to B first?

00:13:45.126 --> 00:13:46.976
But then why does it lead to B?

00:13:46.976 --> 00:13:49.266
Talk to people about it,
have conversations about it,

00:13:49.266 --> 00:13:51.506
and that conversing about it,

00:13:51.506 --> 00:13:55.347
also another way to draw connections.

00:13:55.347 --> 00:13:57.016
You know, I will say that, you know,

00:13:57.016 --> 00:13:59.356
I've always been skeptical
of actually group studying

00:13:59.356 --> 00:14:01.836
that, you know, sometimes
studying is a solitary activity.

00:14:01.836 --> 00:14:04.176
And I actually think, you
know, sometimes group study

00:14:04.176 --> 00:14:07.246
is kind of not as productive
as you would like.

00:14:07.246 --> 00:14:09.936
So, I would always say, your
first goal should be you

00:14:09.936 --> 00:14:12.846
and the material digging deep in it.

00:14:12.846 --> 00:14:15.426
And then, once you, but if
you have some time and space,

00:14:15.426 --> 00:14:17.006
which ideal you have, then it's great

00:14:17.006 --> 00:14:18.676
to be able to talk about it with friends,

00:14:18.676 --> 00:14:20.266
once you've both had a chance

00:14:20.266 --> 00:14:22.066
to kind of struggle around with it.

00:14:22.066 --> 00:14:25.216
And then, I think having
conversation can be really good.

00:14:25.216 --> 00:14:26.049
The other thing I would say,

00:14:26.049 --> 00:14:27.896
is just get in the right frame of mind.

00:14:27.896 --> 00:14:30.576
I think for a lot of
folks, just the stress

00:14:30.576 --> 00:14:32.456
and the anxiety associated with tests

00:14:32.456 --> 00:14:34.516
become the thing that
makes it hard for them

00:14:34.516 --> 00:14:36.316
to take the test or do well on the test.

00:14:36.316 --> 00:14:38.146
And so, I highly recommend meditating.

00:14:38.146 --> 00:14:40.136
We've actually made
meditations on Khan Academy,

00:14:40.136 --> 00:14:42.836
you know, recognize that
your life is so much more

00:14:42.836 --> 00:14:45.006
than a performance on any one test that,

00:14:45.006 --> 00:14:49.216
that, you know, 100 years from
now, no one's going to care

00:14:49.216 --> 00:14:52.326
how you performed on one test or another.

00:14:52.326 --> 00:14:56.196
So, you know, just keep
that in mind that you know,

00:14:56.196 --> 00:14:58.176
a test, it's important to be prepared,

00:14:58.176 --> 00:15:01.026
do your best, but be in the
Zen state as you take it.

00:15:01.026 --> 00:15:02.796
Don't be all caught up with
like, what's gonna happen

00:15:02.796 --> 00:15:04.796
if I do badly on the
test or I have to do well

00:15:04.796 --> 00:15:06.836
if I you know, I want this to happen.

00:15:06.836 --> 00:15:09.436
That's going to be counterproductive.

00:15:09.436 --> 00:15:11.096
Let's see.

00:15:11.096 --> 00:15:13.893
There's from Facebook, Lisa Willtrout,

00:15:13.893 --> 00:15:16.626
"What platform do you recommend
to learn science terms

00:15:16.626 --> 00:15:18.076
for long-term memory?

00:15:18.076 --> 00:15:19.986
Is there an existing flashcard site?"

00:15:19.986 --> 00:15:23.926
So, you know, for flashcard
type stuff, there is Quizlet.

00:15:23.926 --> 00:15:24.759
I recommend it.

00:15:24.759 --> 00:15:26.556
I'm good friends with
the founder of Quizlet.

00:15:26.556 --> 00:15:28.426
And, I'm sure there's people

00:15:28.426 --> 00:15:30.926
who've already created
flashcards on Quizlet

00:15:30.926 --> 00:15:32.356
for whatever you need to learn

00:15:32.356 --> 00:15:34.756
and you can also create
your own flashcards

00:15:34.756 --> 00:15:36.096
and they have some really neat modalities

00:15:36.096 --> 00:15:38.696
where you can, you know,
have kind of flashcards

00:15:38.696 --> 00:15:39.756
but there's other ways to make sure

00:15:39.756 --> 00:15:42.716
that you've essentially
memorized the knowledge.

00:15:42.716 --> 00:15:44.906
I would say Khan Academy is a great place

00:15:44.906 --> 00:15:48.136
for practicing the skills
and the concepts of science

00:15:48.136 --> 00:15:51.026
and a little bit of
the not, the knowledge,

00:15:51.026 --> 00:15:52.656
I guess you could call it
memorization if you like.

00:15:52.656 --> 00:15:53.496
So, if you go to Khan Academy

00:15:53.496 --> 00:15:55.436
for biology or chemistry or physics,

00:15:55.436 --> 00:15:57.366
chemistry and physics don't
have a lot of memorization,

00:15:57.366 --> 00:15:59.386
I would argue even some of the formulas,

00:15:59.386 --> 00:16:01.726
once you understand the
intuition behind them,

00:16:01.726 --> 00:16:03.283
you don't need to memorize
them and you can see it

00:16:03.283 --> 00:16:05.906
and you can even derive some
from some of the others.

00:16:05.906 --> 00:16:07.876
Biology has a little bit of memorization,

00:16:07.876 --> 00:16:09.956
but once again, it's more conceptual.

00:16:09.956 --> 00:16:10.966
You know, in some classes,

00:16:10.966 --> 00:16:13.706
you might have to memorize the
phases of mitosis or meiosis

00:16:13.706 --> 00:16:15.376
or, you know, things like that.

00:16:15.376 --> 00:16:17.466
But, I think Khan Academy
really has your back

00:16:17.466 --> 00:16:18.886
and you know, fingers crossed,

00:16:18.886 --> 00:16:20.606
I'm hoping that in the next few months,

00:16:20.606 --> 00:16:22.146
we have the resources to actually

00:16:22.146 --> 00:16:25.376
really flash out a middle
school and early high school

00:16:25.376 --> 00:16:27.256
science on Khan Academy.

00:16:27.256 --> 00:16:28.426
We actually have high
school science already,

00:16:28.426 --> 00:16:30.426
but really take that to the next level.

00:16:30.426 --> 00:16:32.046
So, you know, stay tuned.

00:16:32.046 --> 00:16:34.016
We already have a lot of
science on Khan Academy,

00:16:34.016 --> 00:16:35.716
both high school and AP level,

00:16:35.716 --> 00:16:38.096
you know, chemistry, biology, physics,

00:16:38.096 --> 00:16:39.646
and we're building out
more of the high school.

00:16:39.646 --> 00:16:41.556
Hope to have middle school soon.

00:16:41.556 --> 00:16:42.949
And obviously, we have
college level 'cause the AP

00:16:42.949 --> 00:16:46.346
and the college level are
essentially the same thing.

00:16:46.346 --> 00:16:47.466
We also have things like econ.

00:16:47.466 --> 00:16:49.486
So, yeah, check out Khan Academy,

00:16:49.486 --> 00:16:50.957
but Quizlet is also a great place

00:16:50.957 --> 00:16:53.029
to make sure you memorize your facts.

00:16:54.266 --> 00:16:56.816
So, from YouTube, Suzanna Garcia Domingues

00:16:56.816 --> 00:16:58.226
and Suzanna, I feel like I know you

00:16:58.226 --> 00:17:00.206
because you are a consistent,

00:17:00.206 --> 00:17:03.506
strong question asker on these,

00:17:03.506 --> 00:17:05.556
I don't even look at the
question when I have a guest on

00:17:05.556 --> 00:17:06.836
and I see it's from Suzanna and I'm like,

00:17:06.836 --> 00:17:07.706
that's gonna be a good question.

00:17:07.706 --> 00:17:10.796
I'm just gonna read it before
I even know what she's asking.

00:17:10.796 --> 00:17:12.853
So Sal asks, so Suzanna asks,

00:17:12.853 --> 00:17:14.746
"Hi, Sal, you're such a happy person.

00:17:14.746 --> 00:17:16.486
Would you have any pet peeves?"

00:17:16.486 --> 00:17:18.666
Well, I'm happy that you
think I'm a happy person.

00:17:18.666 --> 00:17:22.386
I look, I do think for the
most part, I am a happy person.

00:17:22.386 --> 00:17:26.256
But, I have my share of you
know, every now and then,

00:17:26.256 --> 00:17:28.006
I've talked about this, you know, I get,

00:17:28.006 --> 00:17:29.276
I've been anxious on planes.

00:17:29.276 --> 00:17:30.426
I've learned to deal with it.

00:17:30.426 --> 00:17:32.206
Actually, that's what
brought me to meditation,

00:17:32.206 --> 00:17:34.296
which has actually made
me a much happier person.

00:17:34.296 --> 00:17:37.106
'Cause it is easy,
especially when you feel like

00:17:37.106 --> 00:17:38.596
you're working on
something really important.

00:17:38.596 --> 00:17:40.376
And I feel like Khan Academy,
we gotta get this right.

00:17:40.376 --> 00:17:42.216
There's millions of folks depending on us.

00:17:42.216 --> 00:17:44.286
And so, sometimes at
work if you know people

00:17:44.286 --> 00:17:46.826
are disagreeing with something
or something didn't happen

00:17:46.826 --> 00:17:48.596
the way that you wanted,
you know, you get,

00:17:48.596 --> 00:17:50.836
you might get a little
triggered or you go home

00:17:50.836 --> 00:17:52.106
and you lose sleep, you're like wake up,

00:17:52.106 --> 00:17:54.966
you're like, oh, how come
this can't happen faster?

00:17:54.966 --> 00:17:56.196
Why did that person say that?

00:17:56.196 --> 00:18:00.766
Or, and, you know, I've
learned through meditation

00:18:00.766 --> 00:18:02.526
to not even let that happen.

00:18:02.526 --> 00:18:06.366
That when, you know, it's
important to engage on a problem

00:18:06.366 --> 00:18:10.206
and do what you can but then
you need to compartmentalize

00:18:10.206 --> 00:18:12.806
that a little bit and
not let it affect you

00:18:12.806 --> 00:18:14.406
and not let you mull on it.

00:18:14.406 --> 00:18:16.006
And until a lot of times, I, you know,

00:18:16.006 --> 00:18:18.836
to the previous question, I
delegated to my subconscious

00:18:18.836 --> 00:18:22.286
and let my, I say, "subconscious,
you worry about it.

00:18:22.286 --> 00:18:23.273
I gotta sleep."

00:18:24.506 --> 00:18:26.326
And usually when I wake
up the next morning

00:18:26.326 --> 00:18:27.676
or the, you know, a week later, you know,

00:18:27.676 --> 00:18:29.056
the thing that I thought was this problem

00:18:29.056 --> 00:18:30.416
that could have kept me up.

00:18:30.416 --> 00:18:33.206
There's usually a solution,
it usually resolves itself

00:18:33.206 --> 00:18:37.836
or, come up with the
solution and you realize,

00:18:37.836 --> 00:18:39.126
yeah, that was a silly
thing to stress over.

00:18:39.126 --> 00:18:41.836
So once you go through enough
cycles like that in life,

00:18:41.836 --> 00:18:44.426
there's many things that
in high school, college,

00:18:44.426 --> 00:18:45.259
you know, I stressed over?

00:18:45.259 --> 00:18:47.136
Well, I, you know, I'm sure a lot of kids

00:18:47.136 --> 00:18:48.926
are stressed about college admissions now,

00:18:48.926 --> 00:18:51.176
you know, we all had
our childhood crushes.

00:18:51.176 --> 00:18:52.826
Does that person even see me?

00:18:52.826 --> 00:18:54.656
You know, do I have the
guts to walk up to them

00:18:54.656 --> 00:18:55.786
and talk to them?

00:18:55.786 --> 00:18:57.706
You know, will I pick the right major?

00:18:57.706 --> 00:18:59.076
How do I make friends?

00:18:59.076 --> 00:19:00.846
You know, fear of missing out.

00:19:00.846 --> 00:19:02.546
Can I pick the right career?

00:19:02.546 --> 00:19:03.726
How am I going to pay off my debt?

00:19:03.726 --> 00:19:06.226
You know, these are all
stresses and anxieties.

00:19:06.226 --> 00:19:07.566
And did someone say that about me,

00:19:07.566 --> 00:19:08.496
but that's not who I am.

00:19:08.496 --> 00:19:10.296
I'm really a nicer person than that.

00:19:12.096 --> 00:19:14.076
And, you know, over time, you just realize

00:19:14.076 --> 00:19:16.086
that these things that stress you out,

00:19:16.086 --> 00:19:18.606
a week later, six months later
really aren't that important.

00:19:18.606 --> 00:19:19.766
And so, when someone new
and comes up, you're like,

00:19:19.766 --> 00:19:21.559
you know what, six months from now,

00:19:22.526 --> 00:19:24.746
if something needs fixing, I'll fix it.

00:19:24.746 --> 00:19:26.546
If there's some action I
can take that's productive,

00:19:26.546 --> 00:19:27.436
I'll take it.

00:19:27.436 --> 00:19:28.526
But, don't let it bother you.

00:19:28.526 --> 00:19:29.966
And meditation really helps there,

00:19:29.966 --> 00:19:32.366
because meditation, you
know, our minds are normally

00:19:32.366 --> 00:19:34.316
just all over the place.

00:19:34.316 --> 00:19:37.306
You're like, you know, I wonder if a horse

00:19:37.306 --> 00:19:39.186
and a zebra can breed.

00:19:39.186 --> 00:19:41.066
Oh, I gotta pay my taxes.

00:19:41.066 --> 00:19:43.026
Am I a good father?

00:19:43.026 --> 00:19:44.706
That's like five seconds of your brain

00:19:44.706 --> 00:19:48.676
if you just let it, you
know, can I outrun a bear?

00:19:48.676 --> 00:19:50.046
You can't.

00:19:50.046 --> 00:19:53.036
You know, but even those
big, you know, chubby bears,

00:19:53.036 --> 00:19:54.716
no, you can't outrun them, you know.

00:19:54.716 --> 00:19:56.156
So, your brain is like a circus,

00:19:56.156 --> 00:19:59.286
it's all sorts of stuff is
going on from moment to moment.

00:19:59.286 --> 00:20:01.606
And meditation is really
getting it back to a place

00:20:01.606 --> 00:20:05.296
where you're just like this
candle and without wind,

00:20:05.296 --> 00:20:07.466
you know, you're just still
and you're not thinking.

00:20:07.466 --> 00:20:10.566
And it's when you're
in that frame of mind,

00:20:10.566 --> 00:20:12.816
you know, there's nothing, you know,

00:20:12.816 --> 00:20:14.276
this can apply to any religion.

00:20:14.276 --> 00:20:16.976
You know, in every religion
there's been mystics

00:20:16.976 --> 00:20:19.906
who have gotten well-steeped in meditation

00:20:19.906 --> 00:20:21.086
and they've been able to still their mind

00:20:21.086 --> 00:20:23.346
and when you still your mind,

00:20:23.346 --> 00:20:25.706
you start appreciating how silly

00:20:26.626 --> 00:20:29.606
some of the things that you
were taking seriously are

00:20:29.606 --> 00:20:31.236
and you start appreciating that you know,

00:20:31.236 --> 00:20:34.016
at the end of the day, all you really are,

00:20:34.016 --> 00:20:36.076
you know, that you can
really claim you are

00:20:36.076 --> 00:20:38.366
is a consciousness, and
even you know these notions

00:20:38.366 --> 00:20:41.376
of your identity and your name
and your physical persona,

00:20:41.376 --> 00:20:43.036
these are just part of material reality.

00:20:43.036 --> 00:20:46.136
These are, and some of these
things are constructed,

00:20:46.136 --> 00:20:50.276
you know, your persona,
your place in society,

00:20:50.276 --> 00:20:52.003
your connections and that
liberates you a little bit.

00:20:52.003 --> 00:20:54.659
And I see the other reason
I'm happy is I think

00:20:54.659 --> 00:20:56.976
I've just been very lucky in life.

00:20:56.976 --> 00:20:58.976
You know, I get to do what I love.

00:20:58.976 --> 00:21:01.516
You know, I have a wonderful
wife, wonderful kids,

00:21:01.516 --> 00:21:03.476
I get to, you know, work
with wonderful people.

00:21:03.476 --> 00:21:05.576
And I think there's no
there's nothing like that,

00:21:05.576 --> 00:21:08.896
you know, I guess makes
you click your heels

00:21:08.896 --> 00:21:11.189
on the way to work which
is now in my house.

