00:23:20.176 --> 00:23:21.726
So, I do play chess.

00:23:21.726 --> 00:23:25.819
I would consider myself
a very good amateur.

00:23:26.826 --> 00:23:28.946
I played chess, you know, I
first played when I was five.

00:23:28.946 --> 00:23:30.506
My uncles taught me to play.

00:23:30.506 --> 00:23:32.836
I never kind of played competitively.

00:23:32.836 --> 00:23:33.669
I would say, you know,

00:23:33.669 --> 00:23:38.026
that I've had a few
highlights in my chess life.

00:23:38.026 --> 00:23:41.106
There was a event at
one of our funders house

00:23:41.106 --> 00:23:45.356
where Magnus Carlsen showed
up and played a demo game

00:23:45.356 --> 00:23:49.686
against four people
and he was blindfolded.

00:23:49.686 --> 00:23:51.336
And I was one of the four people.

00:23:52.296 --> 00:23:55.406
And, there was a moment and he was playing

00:23:55.406 --> 00:23:57.926
three other people and
and he was blindfolded.

00:23:57.926 --> 00:24:01.026
So, I'm not claiming
that I'm anywhere close.

00:24:01.026 --> 00:24:03.826
But there was a moment
where I guess his visual

00:24:03.826 --> 00:24:06.106
'cause he was clearly
memorizing four boards at once.

00:24:06.106 --> 00:24:09.476
He had, he made a mistake
and I had the advantage.

00:24:09.476 --> 00:24:11.896
I had a rookie advantage
on Magnus Carlsen.

00:24:11.896 --> 00:24:13.516
And that actually made me nervous.

00:24:13.516 --> 00:24:16.136
I was like, I cannot mess
this up at this point.

00:24:16.136 --> 00:24:18.936
And then like, he beat
the other three players

00:24:18.936 --> 00:24:23.906
pretty quickly and then I
just felt his chess brainpower

00:24:23.906 --> 00:24:27.426
completely focused on
me, like crushing me,

00:24:27.426 --> 00:24:30.139
and I just, I just, I choked.

00:24:31.316 --> 00:24:33.576
But, he came up, you know,
took the blindfold off,

00:24:33.576 --> 00:24:36.013
and he kind of came up and he
was kind of like, "Good game.

00:24:36.013 --> 00:24:38.233
You know, I was nervous for a moment."

00:24:39.416 --> 00:24:42.086
That's the peak of by my chess career.

00:24:42.086 --> 00:24:46.199
And, I have to say my son,
my oldest son named Ron,

00:24:47.876 --> 00:24:51.476
he's really into chess and
he has made me a lot better.

00:24:51.476 --> 00:24:54.136
We play couple of times a week

00:24:54.136 --> 00:24:56.056
and I have to give him credit.

00:24:56.056 --> 00:24:58.236
He has a really great
growth mindset about it.

00:24:58.236 --> 00:25:01.633
About a year ago, I would
win about 70% of the game

00:25:01.633 --> 00:25:03.336
and he's quite good as you can imagine

00:25:03.336 --> 00:25:06.756
for an 11 year old considering, you know,

00:25:06.756 --> 00:25:09.196
I had an advantage momentarily
against Magnus Carlsen

00:25:09.196 --> 00:25:11.699
while he was playing blindfolded
and four other people.

00:25:12.712 --> 00:25:17.712
But now, he wins about 60% of
the games and if I beat him,

00:25:18.526 --> 00:25:20.823
he's like, "Okay, give me a few hours."

00:25:20.823 --> 00:25:22.366
And then he goes, you asked about a site,

00:25:22.366 --> 00:25:23.676
he goes to the site called Chess Kids,

00:25:23.676 --> 00:25:24.626
which I think is great.

00:25:24.626 --> 00:25:27.326
You know, it's like a $4
a month type membership.

00:25:27.326 --> 00:25:29.376
Obviously, I'm a big fan of
free world class learning,

00:25:29.376 --> 00:25:32.506
but I think you know, that's a
good deal for learning chess,

00:25:32.506 --> 00:25:34.406
'cause it has really good
exercises and he's learned a lot

00:25:34.406 --> 00:25:36.926
and I'm not on the site, but
he's making me a lot better.

00:25:36.926 --> 00:25:38.646
And sometimes he explains
the new techniques

00:25:38.646 --> 00:25:39.879
that he learns on chess.

00:25:40.986 --> 00:25:43.276
But, I really like that whenever he loses,

00:25:43.276 --> 00:25:44.666
he doesn't like throw a tantrum.

00:25:44.666 --> 00:25:46.266
He's like, "give me a few hours."

00:25:46.266 --> 00:25:49.786
Then he goes on Chess Kids
and he works his skills.

00:25:49.786 --> 00:25:52.293
And then he's like, "Dad, you wanna play?"

00:25:53.436 --> 00:25:55.266
When he has that look,
"Dad, you wanna play?"

00:25:55.266 --> 00:25:57.466
I know I'm about to be crushed.

00:25:57.466 --> 00:26:00.176
And, I'm gonna have to up
level myself a little bit.

00:26:00.176 --> 00:26:02.556
No, 'cause I think it's a wonderful game.

00:26:02.556 --> 00:26:05.146
Very, and you learn
frankly a lot about life.

00:26:05.146 --> 00:26:08.046
You learn a lot about, you know, patience.

00:26:08.046 --> 00:26:09.289
It's a wonderful game.

