00:27:39.666 --> 00:27:44.666
So, Mohammed says, I use your
math videos for meditation.

00:27:44.696 --> 00:27:47.696
Well, you know, math can
be a form of meditation.

00:27:47.696 --> 00:27:49.826
You know, the kind of
classic form of meditation

00:27:49.826 --> 00:27:51.626
is kind of sit still, eyes closed

00:27:51.626 --> 00:27:56.626
and focus on your
breathing and become still.

00:27:56.986 --> 00:28:00.796
But, you know, anytime you're
doing something mindfully,

00:28:00.796 --> 00:28:02.586
and your brain isn't all over the place,

00:28:02.586 --> 00:28:04.076
that's a form of meditation.

00:28:04.076 --> 00:28:05.626
So, if you're in a math problem

00:28:05.626 --> 00:28:08.876
and your brain is kind
of still in that space,

00:28:08.876 --> 00:28:10.726
that sometimes happens in sports,

00:28:10.726 --> 00:28:13.816
it can, you know, happen when
you're listening to music,

00:28:13.816 --> 00:28:17.156
creating something, that's
a form of meditation.

00:28:17.156 --> 00:28:19.866
So, that's the first I've heard of that

00:28:19.866 --> 00:28:21.616
but I'm happy that someone can find

00:28:22.486 --> 00:28:24.086
a Khan Academy video meditative.

00:28:25.876 --> 00:28:28.253
So, Sanu and Abraham says,

00:28:28.253 --> 00:28:29.676
"How can we practice meditation

00:28:29.676 --> 00:28:32.466
or teach with a
hyperactive four year old?"

00:28:32.466 --> 00:28:33.299
I don't know.

00:28:33.299 --> 00:28:36.026
I mean, I've tried to convince
my kids to meditate with me

00:28:36.026 --> 00:28:38.706
and they do, you know, my older
kids do to certain degree.

00:28:38.706 --> 00:28:41.126
The school they go to, the Khan Lab School

00:28:41.126 --> 00:28:44.049
which I helped start, they
have, they do some meditation,

00:28:44.049 --> 00:28:45.386
which I think is really great.

00:28:45.386 --> 00:28:46.966
But, for younger kids, it's just really,

00:28:46.966 --> 00:28:49.816
honestly a four or five
year old, even 30 seconds.

00:28:49.816 --> 00:28:53.136
If they can sit silently
for 30 seconds, it's a win.

00:28:53.136 --> 00:28:55.956
And if you build that habit,
then they'll become a little,

00:28:55.956 --> 00:28:58.726
honestly 10 seconds, start
with 10 seconds and you know,

00:28:58.726 --> 00:29:01.706
give them like an M&amp;M at the end of it.

00:29:01.706 --> 00:29:04.116
But it starts to build a
muscle where you start to,

00:29:04.116 --> 00:29:06.216
you know, you'd be surprised
how many adults have trouble

00:29:06.216 --> 00:29:10.066
just sitting quietly, eyes
closed with themselves,

00:29:10.066 --> 00:29:11.306
with their own mind.

00:29:11.306 --> 00:29:12.626
To some degree, they wanna get distracted,

00:29:12.626 --> 00:29:14.096
so they don't have to deal with everything

00:29:14.096 --> 00:29:16.496
their brain is kind of throwing at them.

00:29:16.496 --> 00:29:18.926
So, for me, you know, I found in the early

00:29:18.926 --> 00:29:20.646
when I was starting to meditate,

00:29:20.646 --> 00:29:22.786
you know, even two, three
minutes your brain is saying,

00:29:22.786 --> 00:29:25.206
oh, somewhat, you know,
there's a lot more outside,

00:29:25.206 --> 00:29:26.846
you can't meditate.

00:29:26.846 --> 00:29:29.236
You're hungry, you know, your back hurts.

00:29:29.236 --> 00:29:30.816
You know, do I itch my nose or not?

00:29:30.816 --> 00:29:32.706
Will that break the meditation?

00:29:32.706 --> 00:29:35.316
But then, you just keep it,
you keep doing it as a habit.

00:29:35.316 --> 00:29:38.686
And now, I do 30 minutes
at least once a day

00:29:38.686 --> 00:29:43.496
and you know, I don't claim
to be some type of, you know,

00:29:43.496 --> 00:29:45.406
person whose mind is always still

00:29:45.406 --> 00:29:47.846
and I've somehow reached Nirvana.

00:29:47.846 --> 00:29:50.946
But there are moments where
I do feel that stillness

00:29:50.946 --> 00:29:53.176
and there is, it almost feels euphoric.

00:29:53.176 --> 00:29:56.436
And time, you know,
when my meditation timer

00:29:56.436 --> 00:29:58.636
on my phone rings, a little chime,

00:29:58.636 --> 00:30:02.311
I feel like the tie, I was
like, oh, wow, you know,

00:30:02.311 --> 00:30:04.846
I was in this inward journey.

00:30:04.846 --> 00:30:08.426
So, it's a muscle that
you can build over time.

00:30:08.426 --> 00:30:11.676
But you know, my five year old, you know,

00:30:11.676 --> 00:30:12.996
we're not like this family
that is like, you know,

00:30:12.996 --> 00:30:15.536
we're a bunch of monks and
we meditate all the time.

00:30:15.536 --> 00:30:18.179
I'm trying, I'm trying
'cause I think honestly,

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:20.596
you know, there's these
stereotypical things

00:30:20.596 --> 00:30:21.616
you wanna instill with your kids,

00:30:21.616 --> 00:30:23.906
you wanna make sure they
have a lot of you know,

00:30:23.906 --> 00:30:27.176
academic capabilities so that
they get you know, a good job.

00:30:27.176 --> 00:30:28.476
You wanna make sure they're good people,

00:30:28.476 --> 00:30:30.696
that they're ethical, that
they have a good work ethic,

00:30:30.696 --> 00:30:33.836
but I think the resilience of meditation

00:30:34.846 --> 00:30:37.046
that you get, you know,
that's the ultimate thing

00:30:37.046 --> 00:30:38.946
because life's gonna throw
curve balls at everyone

00:30:38.946 --> 00:30:40.096
and some folks we're gonna get

00:30:40.096 --> 00:30:41.626
bigger curve balls than others.

00:30:41.626 --> 00:30:46.526
But if folks can always
be grounded, be present,

00:30:46.526 --> 00:30:49.386
have gratitude for what
they have and be mindful.

00:30:49.386 --> 00:30:51.196
You know, they're gonna get through it.

00:30:51.196 --> 00:30:53.786
And so, yeah, I would say keep trying,

00:30:53.786 --> 00:30:55.536
keep trying, eventually it'll work.

